<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Saxe-Lauenburg</h1><p class="st-meta">1296 CE – 1876 CE · capital Ratzeburg</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small north German duchy on the lower Elbe that kept the Saxon ducal title for centuries but lost the electoral vote to its Wittenberg cousins.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>When the Ascanian dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> divided their lands, the younger line received the scattered northern territories around Lauenburg on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ratzeburg">Ratzeburg</a>, formally separated from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-saxe-wittenberg">Duchy of Saxe-Wittenberg</a> in 1296. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-bull-of-1356">Golden Bull of 1356</a> gave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> electoral vote to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wittenberg">Wittenberg</a> rather than Lauenburg, leaving Lauenburg a minor duchy.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The dukes were often in debt and pledged lands, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vierlande">Vierlande</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bergedorf">Bergedorf</a>, to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-and-hanseatic-city-of-lubeck">Lübeck</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/free-and-hanseatic-city-of-hamburg">Hamburg</a>. The estates of the duchy held real power, and the old salt road from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luneburg">Lüneburg</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> crossed its territory. The duchy turned Lutheran in the 16th century. When the Ascanian line died out in 1689, the dukes of Brunswick-Lüneburg seized it.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Lauenburg passed with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/electorate-of-hanover">Electorate of Hanover</a> and in 1815 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>. After the war of 1864 it went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, whose king ruled it in personal union until it was incorporated into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia</a> in 1876. Bismarck later received the honorary title Duke of Lauenburg in 1890.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-saxe-lauenburg.jpg" alt="Duchy of Saxe-Lauenburg" width="320" height="192"><figcaption>Image: Graphic made by Carport · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Lauenburg.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1296 CE – 1876 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ratzeburg</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Magnus I, Francis II, Julius Francis</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Low German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sachsen-Lauenburg, Lauenburg</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1586&amp;focus=duchy-of-saxe-lauenburg" aria-label="Duchy of Saxe-Lauenburg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4919 333 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Saxe-Lauenburg lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5239 589L5237 629M5239 589L5229 694M5239 589L5219 626M5239 589L5274 535M5239 589L5300 622"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5237 629h0M5229 694h0M5219 626h0M5274 535h0M5300 622h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5239 589h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5239 589h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Ratzeburg. <a href="/atlas?year=1586&amp;focus=duchy-of-saxe-lauenburg">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
